favorite days of the  year and I am really pleased to be able  to announce the prize winners we are  honored to be the home of the wyndham  Campbell prizes and literary festival  and we're especially grateful to the  late donald windham and sandy campbell  for creating this legacy this year there  were 61 nominees from 12 countries from  this group nine have been selected to  receive the prize which comes with a  citation diploma and a hundred and fifty  thousand dollars to support their  writing so I will now announce the names  of the 2015 prize winners and i will  read their citations the wyndham  campbell prizes for nonfiction are  awarded to from the united kingdom  Edmund ivalo edmund of all sure  narrative instinct and lyrical  imagination inform a deeply felt  examination of the hold that objects  have on our personal and collective  memory also from the United Kingdom  Geoff Dyer omnivorous Lee curious and  psychologically probing Jeff dyers  writings reinvent again and again the  possibilities of nonfiction discovering  as many new subjects as he does ways of  writing about them from the United  States John Jeremiah Sullivan John  Jeremiah Sullivan's wide-ranging Zubrin  essays engaged a full spectrum of  American life with passion precision and  wit the wyndham Campbell prizes for  drama are awarded to from the United  States Jackie Sibley's drury Jackie  Drury deftly blends historical inquiry  and meta theatrical experiment to  challenge assumptions about race  performance  and individual responsibility from the  United Kingdom Helen Edmondson Helen  Edmonton's ambitious plays to still  historical complexities through  character whose passions and ethical  dilemmas mirror and illuminate a larger  political landscape also from the United  Kingdom Debbie Tucker green pushing  speech and silence to the limit Debbie  Tucker greens plays expose the brutal  choices of individuals bound by the  imperatives of family society and love  the wyndham Campbell prizes for fiction  are awarded to from Nigeria and the  United States teju cole teja coles  peripatetic nerrit narrators like his  prose revel in the possibilities and  limitations of global urbanity  navigating the fine line between choice  and circumstance perception and memory  also from nigeria halen ha bila halen  hey Beulah is that rare combination of  storyteller stylist who challenges  expectations while deepening our empathy  for ordinary people confronting  extraordinary times from South Africa  Yvonne vladislav each Yvonne vitae  slavich's fiction explores the  uncomfortable aftermath of apartheid  through inventive meditations on the  complex intersection of history politics  and art so the prizes will be conferred  at a ceremony and literary festival here  at yell that begins September 28th 2015  this fall all nine Prize winners will  come to yell to participate we thank  these winners for the ways in which  their work has enriched to all of us and  we congratulate them  on this incredible achievement I look  forward to meeting all of them in  September and hopefully seeing all of  you at the festival as well so thank you  all for joining us today and let me  bring back Michael to answer any  questions that you might have thank you  for being here on this special occasion  Michael thank you Peter so if you have  questions if you could please address  them to the microphone there so that  they get picked up on the livestream  we're being watched all over the world  live right now and and so part of this  is for them yes in the back hi my  question is about the festival I wonder  if you can say anything about what plans  are our shape taking shape for September  the wyndham Campbell prize festival has  been an extraordinary addition to our  rich literary events programs here at  Yale and I'm interested in knowing  what's coming up thank you we have yet  to start planning I think everybody is  still recovering from the shock of the  phone call they received last week but  over the next couple of weeks I will be  in touch with all of the prizewinners to  talk to them and get to know them a  little bit and figure out ways that we  can best show them and show their work  to the audience of the festival and so  hopefully we'll be continuing the  traditions that we've we've begun in the  last couple of years doing events around  campus using the drama school and and  and trying to spread the prizewinners  far and wide both you know because of  the categories that they're writing in  but also because of the different ideas  that they're interested in and I think  one of the unique things about the  festival every year is that we always  find people who have interesting ideas  that are coming into the festival and  and Yale is a wonderful place because no  matter what I do you have there's  somebody here who's thinking about it so  thank you any particular stories of that  struck you from the recipients of the  phone calls well you know this year we I  did it a little bit differently in the  past few years I've made all the phone  calls myself the day after we made the  prize selections and this year I started  sending them emails while we still had a  few hours  left we had already made the selections  and we were writing the citations the  the the selection committee writes all  the prize citations as a group at the  end of the day and it's actually a very  arduous process because everybody's  tired from the conversation of the  morning and so in mid-afternoon I just  started sending them emails asking for  their phone numbers would they call me  or send me their phone number and people  started calling me immediately and and  so I started while they were writing the  citations I was getting phone calls  about every 30 minutes from a shocked  prize winner and i would put them on  speakerphone and tell them they had just  one hundred fifty thousand dollars and i  think my favorite one this year i think  we're the prizes are becoming a bit more  well-known now and so i don't think  anybody thought that it was a Nigerian  phishing scam this year which they did  quite a few of them did last year I  think my favorite response this year one  of the prizewinners said took a moment  then she said you know in front of the  whole selection committee she said you  know if this is a practical joke this is  the meanest practical joke anyone has  ever played on me but you know as ever I  mean people i think were no as far as I  could tell nobody had any idea that they  had been nominated they were all very  surprised and very appreciative and and  and really touched by the fact that that  first of all they were on the radar and  second of all that their prize  nomination went through this very long  rigorous process and ended up selecting  them all right fire away can you speak  to the international character of the  prizes is there an effort to scan all  over the english-language world to find  people there is there is I mean we we  have a limited number of nominations  that are available each year only about  20 to 25 in each category so within that  I seek to cast as wide a net as possible  while also maintaining the high  standards of the nominees that we've  come to expect every year so not every  country in the english-speaking world  gets represented every single year but  when they do get represented we try to  make sure that  there's a certain amount of saturation  so that when judges are looking at their  work they're seeing other writers from  those parts of the world and comparing  them to each other but also comparing  them to writers from everywhere else and  and so we do I travel to other countries  I spent two weeks in Australia last  summer meeting a lot of the literary  folks from down there so hopefully  they'll have some solid representation  in the coming years and and I continue  to do that in two different in different  parts of the world anybody else okay  well thank you very much for coming and  congratulations to all of our prize  winners we look forward to meeting all  of you in September see you next year  you
 

